{
  "id": "u09-l06",
  "unit": "u09-past",
  "title": "Was / were",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "In the present, 'is' disappears: **আমি ছাত্র** 'I am a student'. In the past it **comes back** as **ছিল-**:\n\n- আমি **ছিলাম** — I was\n- তুমি **ছিলে** — you were\n- আপনি / তিনি **ছিলেন** — you were / he was (polite)\n- সে **ছিল** — he / she / it was\n\nSo: **আমি ছাত্র ছিলাম** 'I was a student', **আপনি কোথায় ছিলেন?** 'Where were you?', **দোকান বন্ধ ছিল** 'The shop was closed'.\n\nছিল- is also the past of **আছ-**: **আমি বাসায় আছি** 'I'm at home' → **আমি বাসায় ছিলাম** 'I was at home'.\n\n**গত সপ্তাহে** is 'last week'. Without **গত** ('last'), **সপ্তাহে** means 'in a week / per week'.\n\nYou'll also hear the past perfect **-এছিলাম**: **আমি গত সপ্তাহে সিলেটে গিয়েছিলাম** 'I went to (had been to) Sylhet last week'. For now, just recognise it. In speech it's often **গেছিলাম**.\n\n**শিক্ষার্থী** is a gender-neutral word for 'student' (ছাত্র male, ছাত্রী female). You'll see it on school notices and in the news.\n\n**ছিল** is also written **ছিলো**.",
